data.content:
Here's what happened and how to fix it:

## The Problem

In C# 14 / .NET 10, new `Span<T>` conversions cause `array.Contains(num)` to resolve to **`MemoryExtensions.Contains(Span<T>, T)`** instead of **`Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`**.

This works fine in regular code, but **expression trees cannot represent `Span<T>` / `ReadOnlySpan<T>`** operations — they're stack-only types. So the compiled expression tree throws a `NotSupportedException` at runtime.

## The Fix

Force the call to bind to `Enumerable.Contains` by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Cast to IEnumerable<int>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 2: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 3: Call Enumerable.Contains explicitly
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Any of these prevent the compiler from choosing the `MemoryExtensions` span overload, keeping the expression tree representable.
